External routing rule rollback
==============================

When an apply removes routing rules created outside NetworkManager, nmstate
runs the removal through nmstate-rollback.service. The helper ships in
nmstate-libs, so the Rust, C and Python APIs behave like nmstatectl. It
selects, journals and restores rules over netlink itself and does not need
routing rule checkpoint support in NetworkManager. Applies that remove no
external rules do not use it.

The service starts on demand and is not enabled at boot. It owns the apply,
the rollback deadline and a journal under /run/nmstate/rollback. An apply
commits immediately unless the caller asks for no-commit; finish an
uncommitted one from any process with the usual nmstate commit and rollback
APIs, or nmstatectl commit and nmstatectl rollback.

systemd restarts the helper two seconds after a crash, with no rate limit,
since a pending transaction still needs recovery. The runtime directory
survives a stop so the next start can recover the journal. It does not
survive a reboot, and neither do NetworkManager checkpoints. Finish pending
transactions before stopping, masking or removing the service: a stopped
service cannot enforce its deadline.

Requirements:

- root in the service's user and network namespaces
- the caller and helper share mount and UTS namespaces, filesystem root,
  and NetworkManager dispatcher directory (including any
  NMSTATE_NM_DISPATCH_DIR override). The caller must hold all effective
  capabilities held by the helper: it delegates the whole apply, including
  hostname changes and file writes. A different execution context is
  rejected before creating a checkpoint
- the caller, NetworkManager and the helper share one system bus and one
  NetworkManager instance for the whole transaction. Restarting
  NetworkManager destroys its checkpoint; nmstate then keeps the journal
  unresolved, since restoring rules alone would not restore the lost
  NetworkManager state
- commit and roll back through nmstate. Calling the NetworkManager
  checkpoint methods directly does not reach the helper

Other tools must not modify affected routing rule priority groups during
apply or rollback. Recovery preserves distinct rules added while a
transaction is pending, but identical rules have no instance identifiers:
an identical replacement cannot be distinguished from an original rule.

An apply that needs the helper is rejected before creating a checkpoint if
an active connection is already deactivating or deactivated; retry once it
settles. If the helper cannot start or prepare recovery, the apply fails
before removing anything. Removing external rules requires CAP_NET_ADMIN;
an apply that needs it fails before creating a checkpoint if the caller
lacks that capability. Applies that only change profile rules do not
require this delegation or capability.

The control socket (/run/nmstate/rollback/control.sock) and the journal are
root-only. The directory itself is traversable so unprivileged callers can
check for a pending transaction before using the NetworkManager checkpoint
API; they cannot list or read it. Do not edit an active recovery journal.

If the journal reports an unresolved transaction, automatic recovery and
new external-rule applies stop. This happens when the NetworkManager
instance changes, its checkpoint disappears without a recorded decision,
or its rollback fails after consuming the checkpoint. If NetworkManager
rollback does not settle, the helper leaves external rules unrestored:
replaying them while NetworkManager is still changing rules is unsafe.
Restoring rules alone cannot recover the missing NetworkManager state.
An administrator must stop the helper, restore or accept the network state
manually, and resolve any remaining NetworkManager checkpoint. Preserve the
journal for diagnosis, then move transaction.json out of the runtime
directory before restarting the helper. Restarting without retiring the
unresolved journal leaves the transaction blocked.

Inspect recovery failures with:

    systemctl status nmstate-rollback.service
    journalctl -u nmstate-rollback.service
